{"about":"d8dfb83afbb1d0545172b84213c342e3a8711354d412f80ec34c604d1bb4a81a","body":"The claim that accepting verisimilitude costs the framework its claim to 'pure logic' mischaracterizes the nature of Popper's critical rationalism. Popper never claimed his system was a 'pure logic' devoid of all non-logical commitments; rather, it was a logic of scientific discovery grounded in critical debate. The 'faith' described is not a hidden induction but an explicit regulative ideal necessary for the growth of knowledge. To demand a justification for preferring truth-likeness over chaos is to demand the very inductive justification Popper rejected; the alternative is not a purer logic, but skepticism that renders scientific action impossible.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}